      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let me break it down for you in a more straightforward way.\n\nThe voiced /z/ and unvoiced /s/ sounds are produced by the same mechanism, but with a difference in the vocal cord vibration. In both cases, air flows through the glottis (the space between the vocal folds) and is then directed towards the alveolar ridge (the bumpy part of the tongue just behind the upper front teeth).\n\nFor /z/, the vocal folds vibrate, causing the air to vibrate with a low-pitched hum. This vibration is due to the tension in the vocal folds, which is increased by the airflow. The vibration is also influenced by the shape of the vocal folds and the airflow pattern.\n\nFor /s/, the vocal folds remain still, and the air flows through the glottis without vibrating. This is because the airflow is too fast, and the vocal folds are not able to vibrate. The high-pitched sound is produced by the friction between the air and the alveolar ridge.\n\nIn terms of air pressure, the difference between /z/ and /s/ is that /z/ has a higher air pressure in the glott",
